---
import ResourceLayout from '../../components/ResourceLayout.astro';
---

<ResourceLayout
  icon="💳"
  eyebrow="Insurance and healthcare costs"
  title="Health Coverage"
  introduction="Learn how provincial health insurance works, which services may be covered, and which healthcare costs you may need to pay yourself."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Provincial and territorial health insurance</h2>
    <p>
      Canada has publicly funded healthcare, but each province and territory
      manages its own health insurance plan. Eligible residents usually need to
      apply for a health card before using insured services.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What is usually covered?</h2>
    <p>
      Public health insurance generally covers medically necessary visits with
      physicians, hospital care, diagnostic tests, and many emergency services.
      Exact coverage differs across provinces and territories.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What may not be fully covered?</h2>
    <p>
      Prescription medications outside the hospital, dental care, vision care,
      physiotherapy, counselling, ambulance services, and medical devices may
      not be fully covered. Coverage varies by location, age, income, and health
      condition.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Private or workplace insurance</h2>
    <p>
      Some employers, schools, and private insurance plans help pay for services
      that are not included in public coverage, such as medications, dental
      treatment, glasses, and therapy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Newcomers and waiting periods</h2>
    <p>
      Eligibility rules depend on your immigration status and where you live.
      Some newcomers may need temporary private insurance while waiting for
      provincial or territorial coverage to begin.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Ask about costs before receiving care</h2>
    <p>
      When possible, ask whether a service is covered and whether you will need
      to pay a fee. A clinic, pharmacy, insurer, settlement agency, or provincial
      health authority may be able to explain your options.
    </p>
  </section>

  <aside class="notice">
    <strong>Emergency care should not be delayed</strong>
    <p>
      In a life-threatening emergency, call 911 or go to the nearest emergency
      department. Questions about insurance or cost should not delay urgent care.
    </p>
  </aside>
</ResourceLayout>